      "title": "A waived surrender charge does not waive federal tax",
      "narration": "Change the owner to a forty-five-year-old with a twenty thousand dollar taxable withdrawal and no applicable exception. The additional federal tax on an early nonqualified annuity distribution is generally ten percent of the taxable portion. In this example that is two thousand dollars, in addition to ordinary income tax. An insurer's free-withdrawal allowance concerns its contract charge and does not automatically remove either federal tax. Exceptions to the additional tax include distributions after age fifty-nine and one half, qualifying disability, death and certain substantially equal periodic payments. Use the exception that actually applies. An education or first-home exception associated with an individual retirement account is not a blanket commercial-annuity exception.",
